  <h2>How much does a hiking stove cost?</h2>
  <p>
    Very little. A budget screw-top stove (BRS-3000T type) costs roughly
    A$20-50 and does everything a first overnighter needs. Use it with a small,
    wide pot and shelter it from the wind with your pack or a rock. Pricier
    stoves and integrated systems exist, but you don't need one to cook a
    hot dinner and breakfast.
  </p>

  <h2>How much does an ultralight camp stove weigh?</h2>
  <p>
    For an ultralight camp stove, the stove body itself is only part of the
    weight story. A small canister stove often weighs 70-100 g on its own. Most
    people on an overnighter carry a 100 g (or 110 g) canister, which weighs
    roughly 190-200 g full, and that falls as you burn through the gas. The
    bigger 220-230 g canisters (about 350 g full) are cheaper per gram of gas,
    and often cheaper outright: the Gasmate 230 g canister at Bunnings is about
    A$5, less than many 100 g canisters. On a budget, the extra 150 g is an
    easy trade, and one can lasts several overnighters. Alcohol and
    solid fuel stoves usually weigh less than 50 g including the stand, but you
    still carry fuel separately, and you need more metho by weight than gas for
    the same amount of cooking.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you get into ultralight hiking later, compare total system weight for
    your trip length, not just the empty stove: stove, fuel for every planned
    meal, a pot, and a way to shield the flame from wind.
  </p>

  <h2>How much gas do you need for an overnighter?</h2>
  <p>
    As a rule of thumb, boiling 500 ml of water uses about 5-8 g of gas. A solo
    overnighter (dinner, a hot drink and breakfast) needs only a few boils, so a
    100 g canister covers it with plenty to spare and often lasts two or three
    nights. Wind, cold and long simmering all use more, so if you are not sure
    how full a used canister is, take a fresh one.
  </p>

  <h2>Is canister or alcohol better for a light weight camping stove?</h2>
  <p>
    Canister stoves suit most overnight and multi-day trips because they boil
    faster, handle wind better and let you actually cook rather than just heat
    water. Alcohol stoves earn their place on short, simple trips where you are
    boiling water once or twice a day and want to shave every gram, and where
    you accept a longer wait and no real simmer.
  </p>

  <h2>Where can you buy hiking stove fuel in Australia?</h2>
  <p>
    Screw-top butane-propane canisters are the easiest hiking fuel to find in
    Australia. The cheapest we have found is the <a
      href="https://www.bunnings.com.au/gasmate-230g-power-fuel-iso-butane-cartridge_p0442265"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Gasmate 230 g iso-butane canister at Bunnings</a
    > for about A$5. Anaconda, BCF, Rays, Paddy Pallin and other outdoor stores
    stock them too, as do some Kmart stores, Big W, Woolworths and servos. Make
    sure you buy the screw-top type: the long, aerosol-style "lunchbox" butane
    cartridges used for portable cooktops do not fit hiking stoves, which is a
    common beginner mistake.
  </p>
  <p>
    You cannot take gas canisters on a plane, and they often cannot be sent by
    air freight either, so if you are flying to a hike, buy fuel when you land.
    Methylated spirits for alcohol stoves is sold at supermarkets and hardware
    stores nationwide, and hexamine solid fuel tablets are stocked by camping
    retailers. Whichever fuel you choose, buy it before you leave town: fuel is
    far harder to source once you are near a trailhead than in a regional
    centre. Plan your fuel quantity against your <a
      href="/guides/overnight-hike-packing-list/">overnight hike packing list</a
    > rather than guessing on the day.
  </p>

  <h2>Test your stove at home first</h2>
  <p>
    Before your first trip, light the stove in the backyard. Check the canister
    screws on properly (hiking stoves use the standard screw-top thread, often
    labelled Lindal or EN417), practise lighting it with a lighter, and time how
    long a pot of water takes to boil. It is much easier to sort out a problem
    at home than at camp.
  </p>

  <h2>Stove safety basics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Never cook inside your tent or vestibule. It is a fire risk and can build
      up carbon monoxide.
    </li>
    <li>
      Cook on flat, bare ground away from dry grass and leaves.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tall canister stoves tip over easily. Use a pot with a wide base and do
      not overfill it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Do not leave canisters in a hot car, and never try to refill a
      disposable canister.
    </li>
    <li>
      With an alcohol stove, remember the flame can be almost invisible, a
      spill can spread fire quickly, and you should never top it up while it is
      still hot.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tell someone your plans and when you expect to be back before you head
      out.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What is a total fire ban and how does it affect your stove?</h2>
  <p>
    On a Total Fire Ban day, wood fires, metho (alcohol) stoves and solid fuel
    stoves are banned everywhere. Gas is where it gets tricky. In Victoria, the <a
      href="https://www.cfa.vic.gov.au/warnings-restrictions/fire-bans-ratings-and-restrictions/can-i-or-cant-i"
      rel="noopener">CFA</a
    > allows a portable gas stove under strict conditions, including having 10
    litres of water or a hose ready, which is honestly hard for most hikers to
    meet. In NSW national parks, gas stoves are generally not allowed on a Total
    Fire Ban day unless the local park office or <a
      href="https://www.rfs.nsw.gov.au/fire-information/fdr-and-tobans/total-fire-ban-rules"
      rel="noopener">RFS</a
    > says otherwise. Other states have their own rules, so check your park's
    alerts page and the fire authority the day before you go.
  </p>
  <p>
    A simple backup that hikers swear by: pack a no-cook dinner (wraps, tuna,
    salami, cheese) so a fire ban does not wreck your trip. If you are still
    assembling the rest of your kit, our <a href="/build-my-kit/"
      >Build My Kit</a
    > tool can help you plan gear around the season and region you are heading into.
  </p>

  <h2>What else should you carry for cooking on a hike?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A pot sized to how many people you are cooking for, not larger.</li>
    <li>
      A lighter and waterproof matches as backup, since piezo igniters do fail.
    </li>
    <li>
      Wind protection only if your stove's instructions allow it; some canister
      stoves prohibit windshields because they can overheat the fuel canister.
      A rock, log or your pack makes a decent natural windbreak.
    </li>
    <li>Enough fuel for your trip length plus a little spare.</li>
    <li>
      A way to check the current fire danger rating and any bans before you
      leave phone reception.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you want a complete cooking setup rather than assembling pot, stove and
    cutlery separately, our <a href="/gear/aluminium-cookware-set/"
      >aluminium cookware set review</a
    > covers a nested aluminium cook set that includes a folding canister stove.
    Our <a href="/#kits"
      >beginner hiking kits</a
    > can help you fill in the rest of an overnight setup.
  </p>

  <h2>What to do with empty canisters</h2>
  <p>
    Use a canister until it is completely empty, then recycle it. Many councils
    and some retailers accept empty steel gas canisters, so check what your
    local council offers. Do not put partly full canisters in the bin.
  </p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>
  <p>
    A cheap screw-top canister stove and a 100 g canister, or the A$5 Gasmate
    230 g from Bunnings if budget beats weight, is the sensible default for most
    Australian overnight hikers: quick, controllable and
    reasonably light. Alcohol and solid fuel stoves shave grams for simple,
    short trips, and integrated systems are an upgrade you can add later.
    Whatever you carry, test it at home, check the fire danger rating and total
    fire ban status for your route before you leave, and pack a no-cook meal
    just in case.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="faq">
    <details>
      <summary>Can you use a gas stove during a total fire ban?</summary>
      <p>
        It depends on the state. Victoria allows a portable gas stove under
        strict conditions (including 10 litres of water on hand, which is hard
        for hikers). NSW national parks generally do not allow them on a Total
        Fire Ban day. Check your park's alerts page the day before, and pack a
        no-cook dinner as a backup.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What is the best hiking stove for beginners?</summary>
      <p>
        A simple screw-top canister stove suits most beginners. It lights
        easily, boils quickly, lets you simmer and uses fuel that is widely
        stocked. A budget model (roughly A$20-50) is fine for a first
        overnighter.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>How long does a gas canister last for hiking?</summary>
      <p>
        Boiling 500 ml of water uses roughly 5-8 g of gas, so a 100 g canister
        gives you about 10-15 boils. That covers a solo overnighter with plenty
        spare and often lasts two or three nights. Wind, cold and simmering use
        more.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary
        >Are alcohol stoves allowed on hiking trails in Australia?</summary
      >
      <p>
        Alcohol stoves are generally allowed outside fire ban periods, but they
        are banned on a Total Fire Ban day in every state, the same as wood
        fires. Always check current fire restrictions for your route.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>What hiking cookware works with a canister stove?</summary>
      <p>
        A small pot with a wide, stable base works best, since it is less likely
        to tip on a small stove. A nested cook set keeps everything packed
        together. See our <a href="/gear/aluminium-cookware-set/"
          >aluminium cookware set review</a
        > for one nested aluminium option, which also includes a folding canister
        stove.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Do I need a windshield for a hiking stove?</summary>
      <p>
        Wind can slow cooking, but check your stove's instructions before using
        a windshield. Some canister stoves prohibit any windshield because it
        can overheat the fuel canister. Choose a sheltered cooking spot if your
        stove's instructions do not allow one.
      </p>
    </details>
    <details>
      <summary>Can I use the gas cartridges from my portable cooktop?</summary>
      <p>
        No. The long "lunchbox" style butane cartridges do not fit hiking
        stoves. You need a screw-top canister, sold at outdoor stores, Kmart
        and Bunnings (the Gasmate 230 g canister is about A$5).
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
